1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,687
I built a system that answers questions
about five hundred AI interviews,

2
00:00:05,687 --> 00:00:07,250
entirely on my laptop.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,500
Ask it which concerns scientists raise most,
and it counts.

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:16,827
Plain vector search got none of seventeen
counting and comparison questions

5
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:17,225
right.

6
00:00:17,575 --> 00:00:19,525
The graph got thirteen.

7
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:24,448
But the part worth watching is how often my
own checks were wrong, and how I

8
00:00:24,448 --> 00:00:25,050
found out.

9
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,150
Here is the whole story, with the numbers.

10
00:00:28,762 --> 00:00:33,581
The data is five hundred interviews from the
Anthropic Interviewer dataset,

11
00:00:33,581 --> 00:00:36,537
where people describe how they use AI at
work.

12
00:00:36,887 --> 00:00:42,089
A local model reads each transcript and
pulls out tasks, uses of AI, and

13
00:00:42,089 --> 00:00:44,112
concerns, each with a quote.

14
00:00:44,462 --> 00:00:49,237
A second small model checks that every quote
really supports its claim.

15
00:00:49,587 --> 00:00:53,862
Similar names are merged, and everything
goes into Neo4j.

16
00:00:54,212 --> 00:00:58,437
When you ask a question, a router picks one
of a few fixed queries.

17
00:00:58,787 --> 00:01:01,337
No model writes database code.

18
00:01:01,687 --> 00:01:06,691
One important limit: the interviews were run
by an AI and the people chose

19
00:01:06,691 --> 00:01:11,762
to take part, so every count describes this
sample, not workers in general.

20
00:01:12,384 --> 00:01:14,234
Here is why a graph.

21
00:01:14,584 --> 00:01:19,339
Ask for the top concerns among scientists,
and the answer is a ranked list

22
00:01:19,339 --> 00:01:22,809
with counts out of one hundred twenty-five
interviews.

23
00:01:23,159 --> 00:01:28,109
Plain retrieval can only show you a few
similar answers, so it cannot count.

24
00:01:28,459 --> 00:01:33,524
On my forty test questions, it got zero of
seventeen count and comparison

25
00:01:33,524 --> 00:01:34,634
questions right.

26
00:01:34,984 --> 00:01:37,059
The graph answered thirteen.

27
00:01:37,409 --> 00:01:41,734
That is the one place the extra machinery
clearly earns its place.

28
00:01:42,353 --> 00:01:44,453
How good is the extraction?

29
00:01:45,003 --> 00:01:49,303
I asked a bigger model to check ninety of
the extracted facts.

30
00:01:49,653 --> 00:01:54,214
Seventy-eight percent were correct, and
ninety percent if you count things

31
00:01:54,214 --> 00:01:55,878
the person only wished for.

32
00:01:56,228 --> 00:01:59,903
Concerns were the weak spot, at sixty-three
percent.

33
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:02,203
And recall was worse.

34
00:02:02,553 --> 00:02:07,283
On ten transcripts, the graph captured only
about a third of what people

35
00:02:07,283 --> 00:02:08,203
actually said.

36
00:02:08,553 --> 00:02:10,778
So counts here are lower bounds.

37
00:02:11,404 --> 00:02:15,479
So I tried to fix recall with a looser
prompt for concerns.

38
00:02:15,829 --> 00:02:20,401
Coverage went up, but when I checked a
random sample of the new facts, only

39
00:02:20,401 --> 00:02:23,754
forty-nine percent were correct and a third
were wrong.

40
00:02:24,104 --> 00:02:29,024
I raised the verification threshold, kept
about forty percent of them, and

41
00:02:29,024 --> 00:02:33,079
tested on a fresh sample so I wasn't grading
my own homework.

42
00:02:33,429 --> 00:02:35,929
Seventy-six percent were correct.

43
00:02:36,279 --> 00:02:41,181
Concern coverage on those ten transcripts
went from twenty-two to forty-two

44
00:02:41,181 --> 00:02:41,704
percent.

45
00:02:42,333 --> 00:02:45,333
A small decision model routes each question.

46
00:02:45,683 --> 00:02:50,292
It picked the right route on thirty-four of
thirty-six questions, against

47
00:02:50,292 --> 00:02:53,133
thirty-one for a model choosing tools
itself.

48
00:02:53,483 --> 00:02:58,338
Shorter route descriptions were faster but
it gave up more often, so I kept

49
00:02:58,338 --> 00:03:02,158
the longer ones and accepted about two
seconds for routing.

50
00:03:02,508 --> 00:03:06,915
On forty questions written by a different
model, accuracy was about

51
00:03:06,915 --> 00:03:10,533
seventy-five percent, so treat it as good,
not perfect.

52
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:14,975
A language model writing the answer can
invent a quote.

53
00:03:15,325 --> 00:03:20,034
So the server builds the facts itself, lets
the model only phrase them, and

54
00:03:20,034 --> 00:03:23,425
checks every quote and number against the
tool output.

55
00:03:23,775 --> 00:03:27,625
If anything doesn't match, it falls back to
the plain facts.

56
00:03:27,975 --> 00:03:32,624
Across sixty questions, every delivered
quote was real: ninety-eight of

57
00:03:32,624 --> 00:03:33,475
ninety-eight.

58
00:03:33,825 --> 00:03:38,475
The price is that about one answer in eight
falls back to the plain text.

59
00:03:39,113 --> 00:03:43,863
Then I let a small local model use the tool
in ollmcp.

60
00:03:44,213 --> 00:03:49,050
In a long conversation it sometimes skipped
the tool and wrote quotes with

61
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:50,488
made-up interview ids.

62
00:03:50,838 --> 00:03:53,363
That happened in three of five runs.

63
00:03:53,713 --> 00:03:57,806
With thinking switched off and the
conversation cleared before each

64
00:03:57,806 --> 00:04:01,288
question, it relayed the answers faithfully
in both runs.

65
00:04:01,638 --> 00:04:03,888
The server cannot force that.

66
00:04:04,238 --> 00:04:09,080
The lesson is that free chat with a small
model is unvalidated, so keep the

67
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:11,663
validated path for anything you publish.

68
00:04:12,282 --> 00:04:14,207
Five things I would keep.

69
00:04:14,557 --> 00:04:19,282
One, a graph is worth it for counting and
comparing, not for everything.

70
00:04:19,632 --> 00:04:22,457
Two, test any fix on a fresh sample.

71
00:04:22,807 --> 00:04:27,732
Three, say who judged your numbers; mine
were a larger model, confirmed by

72
00:04:27,732 --> 00:04:27,932
me.

73
00:04:28,282 --> 00:04:32,482
Four, check what a model hands you against
the source, every time.

74
00:04:32,832 --> 00:04:35,957
And five, say how much of the data you used.

75
00:04:36,307 --> 00:04:40,132
This was five hundred of twelve hundred
fifty interviews.

76
00:04:40,482 --> 00:04:44,482
The code and every result are on GitHub,
linked below.
